  "response": "**Setup (Bayes' theorem):**\n\n- Prevalence: P(D) = 1/500 = 0.002, so P(¬D) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity: P(+|D) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(−|¬D) = 0.95, so P(+|¬D) = 0.05 (false positive rate)\n\n**Calculation:**\n\nP(+) = P(+|D)·P(D) + P(+|¬D)·P(¬D)\nP(+) = (0.98 × 0.002) + (0.05 × 0.998)\nP(+) = 0.00196 + 0.0499\nP(+) = 0.05186\n\nP(D|+) = [P(+|D)·P(D)] / P(+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 ≈ 0.037794\n\n**Result:** ≈ 3.78%\n\nSo even with a positive test, there's roughly a 96.2% chance the person does **not** have the condition — because the disease is rare, false positives from the 99.8% healthy population (5% of whom test positive) heavily outnumber true positives from the 0.2% who are actually sick.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
